      "title": "Norway plans temporary ban on smart glasses in some public places",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/05/norway-temporary-ban-smart-glasses-public-places",
      "description": "<p>Camera-enabled Meta spectacles are already restricted in UK and US because of fears about covert recording</p><p>Norway is planning a temporary ban on smart glasses in some public places, amid rising global concern at the spread of the AI-enabled recording devices.</p><p>The Oslo government is planning a bill to prevent the use of the camera-enabled spectacles in parks, beaches, museums, shopping centres, schools, daycare centres, healthcare facilities, gyms and public events, although private use would still be allowed.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/05/norway-temporary-ban-smart-glasses-public-places\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-05T16:11:31"
    },
    {
      "title": "Norway proposes temporary ban on AI glasses in public spaces",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/norway-proposes-temporary-ban-on-ai-glasses-in-public-spaces-126100500767_1.html",
      "description": "A trip to the park. A day at the beach. A workout session at the gym. All in public spaces where smart glasses can snap photos, record conversations or stream video online - without people in the line of their lenses and microphones knowing about it.\nNorway's government wants to get a handle on both the promise and peril of AI glasses, and is proposing that parliament enact a temporary ban on them in some places.\nIt's the latest sign of growing concern in many countries about new technologies that are fundamentally changing the way people live. Some governments have banned the use of social media by children and young teens.\nOne effort to rein in the use of AI glasses has run aground - in the home of Silicon Valley.\nCalifornia's governor last week vetoed legislation that would have penalised the use of smart glasses to record people without their permission in places like changing rooms, doctor's offices and other spaces people generally consider private.\nAnd Microsoft last month",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-05T19:26:56"
    },
    {
      "title": "AI glasses face their first major government crackdown",
